# Hostile audit — support-six reciprocal `H5` finite reduction

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Audited note:** `target_c_support6_reciprocal_h5_reduction_2026-09-18.md`  
**Certificate:** `verify_target_c_support6_reciprocal_h5_reduction_2026_09_18.py`  
**Disposition:** PASS WITH EXPLICIT SCOPE LIMITS. C-II remains open.

This audit checks the places where the `H5` reduction could otherwise overstate a finite enumeration as a geometric exclusion.

## 1. The regular-tournament test is used only as a necessary condition

The new local test does not claim that every regular five-ray cross tournament closes as an equilateral pentagon. The direction is only

\[
\text{strict physical pentagon}
\Longrightarrow
\delta_i+\delta_{i+1}<\pi
\Longrightarrow
\text{regular 5-tournament}.
\]

Therefore retaining 48 uniform and 80 wall candidates is conservative. No candidate is removed because of a condition stronger than the proved two-turn inequality.

## 2. The chirotope enumeration is an over-approximation, not a realizability claim

The verifier checks the rank-three Grassmann-Pluecker sign axiom on all `2^15` free sign assignments compatible with the fixed convex pentagon and two interior-point boundary signs. It does not need every surviving abstract chirotope to be realizable. A nonrealizable survivor only enlarges the candidate set, so the stated reduction remains safe.

## 3. Ground `+--++-` is invoked only in the uniform stratum

The distinct-ray ground order lemma is not used on a wall with coincident ground rays. Uniform `H5` has six distinct ground rays and ground cone width `<pi/3<pi`, so the lemma applies there.

On a simple wall, the verifier instead uses the separate duplicate-ray argument: the tied pair has the same sign, and after global reversal exactly one of the remaining four rays is positive. This is the correct replacement for the distinct-ray word.

## 4. The duplicate-ray argument is scoped to simple walls

If the tied pair had opposite signs, it would cancel and leave a `2+2` equilibrium among four other rays. On a simple wall those four rays are distinct and lie in one open semicircle, so equality of their two unit-vector pair sums forces the unordered pairs to coincide, a contradiction.

This proof needs modification if another ground-ray coincidence occurs simultaneously. The note therefore leaves higher-codimension multiple-collinearity strata open instead of applying the simple-wall argument there.

## 5. The list of simple wall triples is complete for the stated labeling

No three non-ground reciprocal points can be collinear by the preceding support-six lemma. If `0,i,j` is collinear and `ij` is a present reciprocal relation edge, then at one physical endpoint the outside anchor and that relation train are projectively parallel, contradicting the bounded-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem. Hence `ij` must be a missing matching edge.

An interior point cannot lie on either boundary ray `01` or `04`. With hull labels `0,1,2,3,4` and interior labels `5,6`, the only free simple ground-collinearity triples are exactly

\[
025,026,035,036,056.
\]

The verifier enumerates every codimension-one adjacency obtained by flipping only the corresponding chirotope sign.

## 6. Deleting the tied matching mate removes the local zero determinant

On a wall `0,i,j`, the relation edge `ij` is missing. At a tied hull endpoint, the collinear mate is deleted before applying the five-ray positions-two-and-four rule. At all other hull vertices the radial order is unchanged across the wall. Thus using either adjacent uniform chirotope to obtain the hull radial order is legitimate.

The regular-tournament checker explicitly rejects any zero determinant among the five present force rays. No hidden parallel present-edge pair is accepted.

## 7. Symmetry quotient is bookkeeping only

The six uniform and ten simple-wall orbit counts quotient by hull reflection fixing ground vertex `0`, interchange of interior labels `5,6`, and global edge-sign reversal. The verifier renormalizes reflected chirotope orientation so the hull remains counterclockwise.

The raw labelled counts `48` and `80` are established before any quotient, so an error in orbit canonicalization could affect only the compact case count, not the underlying finite reduction.

## 8. Independent exact rerun

The final verifier was executed locally with no floating-point operations in the proof logic and returned:

```text
uniform_rank3_extensions 370
uniform_hull_sign_survivors 600
uniform_regular_force_survivors 48
uniform_symmetry_orbits 6
uniform_matching_families 4 [12, 12, 12, 12]
simple_ground_collinearity_walls 316 {(0, 2, 5): 66, (0, 2, 6): 66, (0, 3, 5): 66, (0, 3, 6): 66, (0, 5, 6): 52}
wall_hull_sign_survivors 488
wall_regular_force_survivors 80
wall_symmetry_orbits 10
interior_pair_collinear_survivors 0
PASS: support-six reciprocal H5 branch reduced to 6 uniform and 10 simple-wall exact sign/order orbits
```

## 9. Remaining gap

The finite reduction does not solve the `H5` branch. The sixteen surviving symmetry orbits still have to be tested against the full metric unit-force equations and signed trace identity. Higher-codimension ground-collinearity strata are also not covered. The collapsed support-six branch is untouched.

**Audit disposition:** the stated finite reductions and the elimination of the simple `056` wall are supportable. No stronger exclusion is claimed. C-II remains open.
